			<div class="content" id="post-content-4552368">
									

<p>I really like this.</p>
<p>I'm given this concept some thought before and I've come to the conclusion that, a character in a story does have free will, in a sense, because an author has a chance of making an idea where a character lives a good life, or dies. In this story there was a chance that all of these characters can't change anything and end themselves because the can't handle being fictional. That means that their lives aren't predetermined, and their world is not completely at the whims of another.</p>
<p>I'm sorry if none of this makes sense. I'm a reader, not a writer, so I can't articulate this idea very well.</p>
<p>P.S. I made this idea so to resolve my struggle with the concept of &quot;what if I'm a fictional character&quot;, that happened a long time ago, so if anyone else has the same struggle, I hope I am coherent enough to have helped.</p>
<p>Oh yeah, +1.</p>

